{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update-stage20.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn spent on the last paused question with a predicate that is actually valid, and the fixed execution requested (valid:true).\\n\\nWhy a new probe was needed: that question's earlier harness used `GetConfiguration().Index()` as its convergence predicate, and that accessor returns a constant zero (recorded as a separate verified defect), so its twenty \\\"converged: false\\\" iterations were meaningless. The new harness (draft/assurance_config_content.go) reads only the atomically published configuration content (the same value the captured cluster assertion compares) and each node's log store, which keeps the measurement independent of the broken accessor.\\n\\nProbe: 25 repetitions of the captured upgrade scenario (protocol-2 pair plus a protocol-3 joiner, AddVoter whose future index identifies the configuration entry), one per-round admission event carrying the iteration, entry index and cluster size, per-node start events, 5ms polling for up to 3s for the published membership to include the joiner, then per-node result events with the published content, log-store contents, applied and commit indices, and one round-end event carrying `all_converged`, `any_lag_with_entry`, the maximum wait and the window.\\n\\nComparisons requested (both protection-flavoured, admitted 1:1 per round so the earlier scoping trap cannot recur): every node's published configuration includes the joining voter within the window, and no node publishes the old membership while its own log store already holds the configuration entry — the \\\"stale configuration state\\\" signature the question asks about. New sources record where that published content comes from (`setLatestConfiguration`, the accessor and the configuration-entry processing path).\\n\\nNative local run, flagged in the limitations as not retained evidence: 25 of 25 rounds converged with per-round maximum wait of 0-5ms and `any_lag_with_entry` false, so no evidence of a stale published membership in the reproduced scenario. The question therefore stays **paused** rather than closed — the disposition is left open until the controller's receipt exists — with explicit notes that the earlier local failure of the captured test (a peer mismatch in about two of three isolated runs) remains unexplained and is not attributed, and that under instrumentation the same scenario converges in milliseconds.\"}"}}
